---
import './misses.css';
---
<figure class="miss-figure">
 <div class="figure-heading"><span class="figure-number">Figure 2</span>A caller leaves; its loader keeps running</div>
 <div class="scene">
  <div class="duration-chart">
   <div class="duration-scale"><span>0 ms</span><span>50 ms</span><span>100 ms</span></div>
   <div class="duration-row"><strong>Caller A · waits 0–50 ms</strong><div class="duration-track"><span style="left:0;width:50%"></span></div><small>Deadline at 50 ms → failure</small></div>
   <div class="duration-row"><strong>Caller B · waits 20–100 ms</strong><div class="duration-track"><span style="left:20%;width:80%"></span></div><small>Receives P7 at 100 ms</small></div>
   <div class="duration-row"><strong>Shared loader L1 · works 0–100 ms</strong><div class="duration-track"><span class="work-bar" style="left:0;width:100%"></span></div><small>Still occupies one source slot after A leaves.</small></div>
  </div>
  <ol class="trace">
   <li><time>0 ms</time><div><strong>A starts L1</strong><span>L1 has its own 120 ms work budget. A’s shorter deadline is not L1’s cancellation signal.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>20 ms</time><div><strong>B joins L1</strong><span>B can wait until 140 ms. Two callers now depend on the same work.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>50 ms</time><div><strong>A stops waiting</strong><span>A’s waiter is removed. L1 and B remain. A starts no replacement read.</span></div></li>
   <li><time>100 ms</time><div><strong>L1 finishes</strong><span>B receives the answer. The source slot and in-flight entry are released.</span></div></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="explanation">One source read, one answered caller, one timed-out caller. A’s timeout did not stop the source read.</p>
 </div>
 <figcaption>A separate invented two-caller trace, using the same P7 key and unchanged card as Figure 1. No permitted old copy is available. L1 finishes within its own budget; the prose below considers what happens when it does not.</figcaption>
</figure>
